== Elliptic curves

Consider ellipse $x^2 / a^2 + y^2 / b^2 = 1$, the circumference is #display-equation($4 integral_0^a sqrt(1 + frac(b^2 x^2, a^2\(a^2 - x^2\))) d x = 4 a integral_0^(pi / 2) sqrt(1 - e^2 sin^2 theta) d theta$)

#statement("Definition")[
The elliptic integral of the #emph[first] kind is #display-equation($integral_0^phi frac(d theta, sqrt(1 - k^2 sin^2 theta))$) Let $t = sin theta$, $x = sin phi$, we have #display-equation($integral_0^x frac(d t, sqrt(\(1 - t^2\)\(1 - k^2 t^2\)))$) The elliptic integral of the #emph[second] kind is #display-equation($integral_0^phi sqrt(1 - k^2 sin^2 theta) d theta$) Let $t = sin theta$, $x = sin phi$, we have #display-equation($integral_0^x sqrt(1 - k^2 t^2) / sqrt(1 - t^2) d t$) The elliptic integral of the #emph[third] kind is #display-equation($integral_0^phi frac(d theta, \(1 - n sin^2 theta\)sqrt(1 - k^2 sin^2 theta))$) Let $t = sin theta$, $x = sin phi$, we have #display-equation($integral_0^x frac(d t, \(1 - n t^2\)sqrt(\(1 - t^2\)\(1 - k^2 t^2\)))$) These elliptic integrals are called #emph[incomplete], they are #emph[complete] if $phi = pi / 2$

]
#statement("Theorem", title: [Legendre's relation])[
For $k^2 + k' ""^2 = 1$, $E\,E'$ are corresponding complete elliptic integrals of the second kind, $K\,K'$ are corresponding complete elliptic integrals of the first kind, then they satisfy the #emph[Legendre's relation]#index-entry("Legendre’s relation") #display-equation($K E' + K' E - K K' = pi / 2$) Equivalently #display-equation($omega_1 eta_2 - omega_2 eta_1 = 2 pi i$) $omega_1\,omega_2$ are the periods of Weierstrass $℘$ function, $eta_1\,eta_2$ are the quasiperiods of Weierstrass zeta function

]#label("Legendre's relation")
#statement("Definition")[
An #emph[elliptic integral]#index-entry("Elliptic integral") is of the form #display-equation($integral_c^x R (x \, sqrt(P\(x\))) d x$) Here $R\(x\,w\)$ is a rational function of $x\,w$ and $P\(x\)$ is a polynomial of degree $3$ or $4$. Every elliptic integral can be reduced into elliptic integrals of the first, second and third kinds

]
#statement("Definition")[
An #emph[abelian integral]#index-entry("Abelian integral") is of the form #display-equation($integral_(z_0)^z R\(x\,w\)d x$) $R$ is a rational function of $x\,w$, and $F\(x\,w\)= 0$ for some #display-equation($phi_n\(x\)w^n + dots.h.c + phi_0\(x\)= 0$) $phi_i\(x\)$ are rational functions of $x$. It is called a #emph[hyperelliptic integral]#index-entry("Hyperelliptic integral") if $F\(x\,w\)= w^2 - P\(x\)$ for some polynomial $P$, note that if degree of $P$ is $3$ or $4$ than it is an elliptic integral

]
#statement("Definition")[
$C$ is a compact algebraic curve of genus $g$, $H^0\(X\,K\)= bb(C)^g$ is generated by one forms $omega_1\,dots.h.c\,omega_g$, $K$ is a canonical bundle, the #emph[Abel-Jacobi map]#index-entry("Abel-Jacobi map") is #display-equation($J : C & arrow.r J\(C\)= bb(C)^g\/Lambda\
P & mapsto (integral_(P_0)^P omega_1 \, dots.h.c \, integral_(P_0)^P omega_g) #h(0.444em) mod med Lambda$)

]
#statement("Theorem", title: [Abel-Jacobi theorem])[
Abel-Jacobi map $J$ is an isomorphism

]
